/* V59 — the titan fights an empty room.

   `02.part` gates the spawner on `if(!game.boss && game.spawnTimer<=0)`, so while a titan is alive
   **every normal spawn is suppressed**. The boss then has to carry the entire tap-density budget on
   its own, and it cannot: a titan is one target with a handful of weak points, against a player
   tapping several times a second. The set-piece the whole campaign builds toward is the emptiest
   screen in the game.

   This is the same fault V56 and V57 addressed for ordinary waves, in the one place where it is most
   visible — and it is worth being precise that it is *only* this one. The research agent also
   reported that `spawnTimer` is set to `Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER` once a wave quota is met, halting
   spawns entirely and punishing good play with silence. That is not true: `checkWave` sets
   `objectiveDone`, awards the directive bonus and schedules `nextWave` 750ms later, and never touches
   the spawn timer. The `MAX_SAFE_INTEGER` writes are duel-mode parking in 03y, where the schedule is
   server-authoritative and the local spawner must not fire alongside it, and save-migration sentinels
   in 03f. Checked before acting, and not acted on.

   Adds during a titan are deliberately thin. The titan is the fight; this is texture around it, and
   the failure mode to avoid is a boss that is really a wave with a large target in the middle of it.
   So: a slower cadence than a normal wave, a hard ceiling on how many can be alive at once, and no
   heavy species — the sentinel and the raider both demand enough attention to pull the player off
   the weak points, which is the boss's own job to do. */

const V59={
 /* Fraction of the normal arrival rate.

    First set to .45 on the reasoning that a titan fight wants texture rather than a second wave.
    Measured, that left the arena empty 58% of the time against 100% before -- a real improvement but
    still more dead air than a set-piece should have, and a peak of only two adds on screen. Raised
    until the boss arena stops being quiet without the adds becoming the fight. */
 rate:.8,
 /* Hard ceiling on adds alive at once, independent of rate. Without this a player who ignores the
    adds to focus the weak points -- which is a legitimate and skilful line -- would drown in them. */
 cap:5,
 /* Scouts and wraiths only. Both die to a single tap and neither has a mechanic that competes with
    the boss for the player's attention. */
 pool:['normal','normal','shard']
};

let v59Timer=0;

const v59Update=update;
update=function(dt){
 v59Update(dt);
 if(!game.running||game.paused)return;
 /* Duels replay one server-authoritative spawn list so both arenas match exactly. Same line V21,
    V35 and V56 drew, for the same reason. */
 if(game.mode==='duel')return;
 if(!game.boss){v59Timer=0;return;}
 /* The hush is deliberate emptiness and this must not fight it. */
 if(game.v35Hush>0)return;

 let live=0;
 for(const o of game.orbs)if(o.y>0)live++;
 if(live>=V59.cap)return;

 v59Timer-=dt;
 if(v59Timer>0)return;
 /* Derived from the base spawner's own cadence rather than a fixed number, so the boss arena keeps
    following the wave ramp instead of drifting away from it as the run gets harder. */
 const base=Math.max(.22,1.05-game.wave*.035);
 v59Timer=base/V59.rate;
 spawnOrb(V59.pool[Math.floor(rng()*V59.pool.length)]);
};
